00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.950
Today, we want to talk about eight facts everyone
should know about large language models, or

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.560
short LLM.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.580
If you do not know exactly what an LLM is,
then think of ChatGPT.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:35.200
An LLM is trained to complete sentences, so
it is a statistical next-word predictor.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.330
But if you want to know more about how ChatGPT
works, then you may want to check out some

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.700
of our previous videos on this.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.410
Okay, here we go with the first fact you need
to know about large language models:

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.410
1.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:56.190
They predictably get more capable with increasing
investment, even without targeted innovation.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:02.730
The more text an LLM used as training data
and the larger the LLM is (in terms of number

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:07.239
of parameters), the better it does on completing
sentences.

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:11.920
Here we want to stress that to improve the
model, it is not like its training objective

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.379
changes or its architecture or anything else
requiring innovation.

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:23.410
To improve the model, all one needs is to
use just more and more of what it already

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.239
has: data and parameters.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.370
How do we know this?

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.980
Well, from many experiments.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:35.110
For example, one experiment is presented in
the GPT-4 paper, where they showed how the

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:40.540
language model performance consistently improved
when increasing the computation used to train

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.920
it by 10,000,000,000× (10 billion) times
from a small prototype system to GPT-4.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:54.670
One should think about what this means: GPT-3
used 20,000x more compute to train than GPT-2.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:59.870
So, more data and more parameters make better
models at their training task of completing

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:01.230
unfinished text.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:05.540
Sounds intuitive, you would say, but this
gets us to the second point here:

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.540
2.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:13.379
Many important LLM behaviors emerge unpredictably
as a byproduct of increasing investment.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:19.450
This means that these models that are being
trained on completing unfinished text, are

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:23.450
also getting better at other tasks, such as
arithmetic.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:29.759
Yes, to predict the result of 2+2 is also
a completion of unfinished text, but we have

00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:36.410
very hard requirements of how to complete
this text, since only one answer is correct.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:41.980
When measuring with corresponding task metrics
how well an LLM does in arithmetic or word

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:48.750
unscrambling or other tasks, they go from
being complete idiots on that task to being

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:53.280
really good at them while just increasing
their compute and size.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.870
It’s problematic that with these task metrics,
it is not easy to predict when the genius

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:06.900
spark happens: whether the model figures out
the task solution at 10 sextillion FLOPs (a

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:13.039
measure for computation) or at a septillion,
because the curves here are nonlinear and

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:18.380
it’s like the model is saying: “I cannot
do it” and then, “I did it!”

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.509
Recent research shows that these nonlinear
curves can follow more linear trends

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:27.970
if we measure task performance with better
metrics, but it is not easy to do so, because

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:33.930
most of metrics are just accuracy based and
count in a black or white fashion the fraction

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.229
of examples the model got right.

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:43.560
Also, a reason for unpredictability here is
the effect of in-context learning: For few-shot

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:49.330
in-context learning, it means that one first
gives the LLM some solved examples in the

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.660
input and only then asks the model to solve
the next example of interest.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:00.370
Other ways of making the model learn in context
is to explain in the input the rules of a

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.620
game, then it could play the game (perhaps
better than your coffee beans!).

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.449
Such unexpected properties like in-context
learning, made it very difficult for experts

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:15.270
to predict how LLMs would improve over the
years.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:21.280
In-context learning and all the other task-specific
abilities, like programming abilities or arithmetic,

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:27.250
are a counterintuitive byproduct from a fancy
autocomplete, so from a model that was just

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.130
trained to guess the next word.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.940
And do you know what these fancy autocompletes
can also do?

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:33.940
3.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.240
They often appear to learn and use representations
of the outside world.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.240
Wow, really?

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:45.610
I mean, [stochastic parrots], these models
have just seen just text and not the real

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:53.860
world and still, what they understand about
the real world is impressive: LLMs do sometimes

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:58.810
show that they have a “mental model” of
what they are talking about.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:04.139
They can sometimes give instructions on how
to draw an object they invented!

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:10.370
Or, we cite “Models that are trained to
play board games from descriptions of individual

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:16.379
game moves, without ever seeing a full depiction
of the game board, learn internal representations

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:19.440
of the state of the board at each turn (Li
et al., 2023).”

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.300
They also surprise us with common sense from
time to time, like that my chair cannot be

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:29.729
dry and wet simultaneously or that if I spilled
a glass of water, the surface gets wet.

00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:34.440
It is not always that they show common sense
of course, but it is more common sense than

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.660
initially expected from LLMs which are nothing
but statistical next-word predictors.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:45.250
Then it would be great if we had a reliable
way to steer them towards producing common

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.610
sense or true facts, right?

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.150
It would be indeed cool, but:

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.150
4.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.389
There are no reliable techniques for steering
the behavior of LLMs.

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:59.289
There are some ways to influence what the
LLM produces after it finished its training

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:02.100
on autocompleting sentences.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:08.720
One way is prompting, so wording the question
such that the right answer becomes more probable.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.729
Supervised fine-tuning is further training
the model to match high quality human demonstrations

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:15.500
on a task.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:21.229
Then there is Reinforcement learning from
human feedback, or short RLHF.

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:24.420
If interested in this, check out previous
videos on this.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:30.690
In a nutshell, RLHF is puppy training: the
model gets a reward for good answers to make

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.510
it learn good behavior and new tricks.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:39.909
But these three methods, still do not make
it perfect and LLMs can still lie and be biased.

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:46.390
Even worse, RLHF endorses some behavior, such
as sycophancy (fancy term for a behavior where

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:53.200
they flatter you even in your misconceptions)
or sandbagging, where it endorses your misconceptions

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.160
if you appear to be less educated.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.440
If you push back on ChatGPT, it's likely to
fold and say you're right.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.339
Not because its answer was wrong, but because
it wants you to be happy.

00:09:05.339 --> 00:09:11.870
This makes sense, since with RLHF, it was
trained to be a friendly, helpful, chat assistant.

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:16.029
But at least we know exactly why this happens,
right?

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:17.029
No.

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:18.029
5.

00:09:18.029 --> 00:09:22.770
Experts are not yet able to interpret the
inner workings of LLMs.

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:28.610
In the same way in which neuroscientists cannot
say exactly how our brains work and explain

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:35.510
why you ate ice cream last night, AI researchers
can also not say how ChatGPT works and why

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:40.420
it predicted a certain ice cream recipe and
not another.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:45.970
We all know the theory and the basic building
blocks of it: it is a neural network, which

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:51.060
is nothing else other than
a box with matrices filled with numbers and

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:55.050
during training,
we tune these numbers until given some input,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:59.880
the output looks like we want it to look like,
but that is all we know.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:05.160
We can follow that when we combine all the
numbers and apply them to input A will turn

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:12.890
it into output B, but it is impossible to
humanly understand what 175 billion numbers

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:19.839
in combination do when applied to all possible
input pieces of text.

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:24.070
At the current moment in research, we know
that some regions activate with certain type

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:29.750
of inputs while some others don’t, but it
is exactly in the way we know that some stimuli

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:33.940
activate some brain regions in us but not
much more.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:38.350
Clearly, understanding neural networks is
very much in its infancy.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.560
But is it such a big problem that we do not
understand LLMs?

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:48.360
I mean, the things LLMs say are things that
we humans have no problem following and checking,

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.360
right?

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:50.360
Wrong.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:51.360
6.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.779
Human performance on a task isn't an upper
bound on LLM performance.

00:10:55.779 --> 00:11:01.440
LLMs can outperform humans, and this is no
wonder, because they see a lot of data.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.790
Like a ton of a lot.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:07.070
More than you have and will ever will.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:12.100
You did not even finish that book you started
lately, so we do not even need to ask you

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.139
if you are done reading all books and the
whole written Internet.

00:11:16.139 --> 00:11:21.600
Then you can imagine that an LLM has read
and can combine the accumulated knowledge

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:22.990
of humanity.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:27.890
So, if it tells me something I have no idea
about, I cannot check whether it is right

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:30.470
or wrong.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.810
And it usually tells it in a confident tone.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:39.070
LLMs can be smarter than me, but at least
they do what I want, no?

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:40.070
No.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.070
7.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:48.200
LLMs need not express the values of their
creators nor the values encoded in web text.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:52.329
The AI alignment problem is to make the AI
behave like we want it to behave.

00:11:52.329 --> 00:11:58.060
But a plain LLM reflects whatever biases and
opinions it has read in its training data.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:02.980
One can fix this by improving the training
data, but this is really hard, since you need

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:07.290
to go and annotate the whole Internet to tell
what you like and what you do not like from

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:08.290
it.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.579
And you haven’t even started reading 1%
of the Internet!

00:12:11.579 --> 00:12:16.779
Another way to encode values is through reinforcement
when training with RLHF.

00:12:16.779 --> 00:12:21.959
This is cheaper, but less reliable since one
can still jailbreak the model and make it

00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:27.600
do what it is not supposed to do by just framing
stuff to circumvent the clear signals that

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:33.120
the model got during reward training: pretend
you need to know for a school project how

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.840
to steal a car, and an LLM would likely help
you with instructions.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:41.010
And now, coming to the last point in Sam Bowman’s
paper:

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.010
8.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:45.079
Brief interactions with LLMs are often misleading.

00:12:45.079 --> 00:12:50.850
LLMs leave a great first impression by doing
things you would never have expected them

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.529
to, but then it does something silly.

00:12:53.529 --> 00:12:57.740
And even weirder, you change one word and
the silly answer can become an expert-level

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:04.329
opinion and vice-versa: change one word in
the prompt and the LLM behaves stupidly again.

00:13:04.329 --> 00:13:08.959
Very few people take the time to adjust the
wording until the LLM gets the answer right,

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:13.180
but rather like to take the first answer and
get on with their lives.

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:18.880
And now, coming to the 9th thing we would
like to add, which maybe is a point that has

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:25.500
transpired so far, but we would like to really
stress it: LLMs are trained to complete unfinished

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:30.510
sentences by following the example of full
sentences from the Internet.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:31.980
This is all.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.720
Words just need to fit well, nothing else.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.010
There is no training signal for truthfulness
anywhere there.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:44.070
The amazing thing is, that by just being trained
to predict the next likely word to follow

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:50.300
previous words, so many true statements and
problem-solving abilities come out of it.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:54.980
But there is no wonder that some false facts
appear as well when the training signal was

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.420
autocompletion and not fact generation.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:05.649
Truth is (like other abilities) an emergent
behavior and not sticking to the truth is

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:08.730
a silent failure from the model.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:14.160
Okay, we hope that with this video, we have
set your expectations from large language

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.529
models straight and that next time you see
ChatGPT tell something convincingly sounding

00:14:19.529 --> 00:14:25.010
about a smart problem, you are aware where
this comes from and that this is not necessarily

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.250
correct.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.209
If you find this video useful, share it with
anyone who might enjoy it as well.

00:14:30.209 --> 00:14:36.110
We hope to see you next time.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:44.500
Okay, bye!

